    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1415">

                <pb facs="0934" n="818" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg818"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.52/311.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Spanish Ambassador to the
                            Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Spanish Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-06-04">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 4, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary</hi>. I have the honor to inform your
                    excellency that according to reports I have received by wire from the Minister
                    of Spain to Mexico, the revolutionists are laying, at Monterey and other places,
                    as they have done at Tampico, on the Spaniards and Mexicans burdens from which
                    other foreigners are exempt.</p>
                <p>I am advised that around Monterey the Constitutionalists are pretending to make
                    arrests with the purpose of demanding ransom from the Spaniards.</p>
                <p>I bring these facts to your excellency’s knowledge and again call your attention
                    to the discrimination shown against my fellow countrymen by the
                    Constitutionalists, for which treatment there is no warrant as the Spaniards, as
                    I have repeatedly had the honor to tell your excellency, have not meddled in the
                    external politics of Mexico, nor am I informed that there is any proof of their
                    guilt in that respect.</p>
                <p>I therefore once more ask your excellency kindly to use your good offices toward
                    remedying a state of affairs that is so unjustified and is causing serious
                    losses to the Spaniards whose conduct entitles them to the same respect as is
                    shown to other foreigners and avail [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Juan Riaño</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
